# 1. Integrate what conflicted

For each unintegrated branch, in the order listed, cherry-pick its commits one
at a time, oldest first, so that every commit keeps its own kind:

    BRANCH=<the branch>
    for COMMIT in $(git rev-list --reverse "$(git merge-base HEAD "$BRANCH")..$BRANCH"); do
        git cherry-pick "$COMMIT"
    done

When `git rev-parse --verify "$BRANCH"` fails, the branch is not in this
clone: fetch it from its `branch.bundle` under `{{ inputs_dirname }}/` with
`git fetch --no-tags <path to branch.bundle> "+$BRANCH:$BRANCH"`.

Resolve each conflict by the reviewed intent (the reviewer's outcome for the
branch says what each unit's witnesses must assert); both sides of a
scaffolding conflict usually belong in the tree until step 2 collapses them.
Keep the original commit message and record each resolution under
`conflicts_resolved`. When a branch cannot be integrated, run
`git cherry-pick --abort`, leave it out, and record an escalation naming its
units.

# 2. Anneal

Each normalization is its own `[NORMALIZE]` commit, verified before the next,
recorded under `normalizations` with its hash, and keeps every witness marker.

- Collapse scaffolding that two or more mappers created independently:
  fixtures, helpers, factories, and setup sequences that do the same thing
  under different names become one definition in the conventional place
  (`conftest.py` or `testing.py`), with every user switched onto it. The
  reviewed outcomes' `scaffolding_added` lists are the starting point; the
  setup guide (below) names what already existed. Collapse only when one
  definition is clearly better than two, and extract only scaffolding, never
  a test's core action (the observable behavior its unit is about), which
  stays written out in the test.
- Parametrize families of near-identical tests: tests with the same setup
  and action shape that differ only in data become one parametrized test.
  Keep every `partial=` note; when the cases witness different units, put
  each unit's marker on its case with
  `pytest.param(..., marks=pytest.mark.witnesses(...))`.
- Triage `{{ fixme_tag }}` markers, which mappers leave at shared-setup
  blockers (`grep -rn "{{ fixme_tag }}" <test roots>`). Make the shared
  change where you can verify it by running the affected witnesses, and
  remove the marker. Where it needs something this environment lacks, or you
  cannot verify it here, leave the marker and record an escalation naming
  the affected units. No marker may remain without an escalation.

